seg-25593339aeb0 = <b>Known causes of false positives</b>, which the share rule and the thresholds reduce but do not remove: vessels in rivers, canals and ports close to a coarse coastline (the { "2" } km inland buffer exists for this); two vessels sharing one identifier, which looks like jumps; transponders with a bad fix or test equipment; and loitering by pilot boats and search vessels, which can resemble circles. A coastline file at { "1" }:{ "10" } million is accurate to about a kilometre.

# text
seg-ac661440a29a = The jump and speed detectors compare reports within one input file; a track split across files or days is not stitched.

# text
seg-05f789b26904 = <b>Memory.</b> Input is read line by line and never held whole. ADS-B keeps only per-aircraft per-cell counters. AIS keeps each accepted report in a compact record (about { "40" } bytes) until its day is evaluated, so memory grows with the number of AIS reports in the input; split a very large AIS input by day.

